  <static seed="51" source="Library/Mizzou/Algebra/exponents_rational/conv_exp_to_exp_two_term_div_02.pg">
    <statement><p>Simplify and write the following as a power of <m>x</m> with a single rational exponent.</p>
<p>If <m>\displaystyle \frac{x^{3/4}}{x^{5/3}} = x^m</m> then <m>m =</m> <fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>-{\frac{11}{12}}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>Recall that <m>\displaystyle{\frac{x^a}{x^b} = x^{a-b}}</m></p>
<p>So, in this case we will need to subtract the exponents,</p>
<p><m>\displaystyle{ \frac{3}{4} - \frac{5}{3} = {-{\frac{11}{12}}}}</m>.</p>
<p>Therefore,</p>
<p><m>\displaystyle \frac{x^{3/4}}{x^{5/3}} = x^{{-{\frac{11}{12}}}}</m></p>
<p>which  means <m>m = {-{\frac{11}{12}}}</m>.</p>
</solution>
